From 2f555aef2fb1a3718ad93e8f3f331216342f8a39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:36:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 92/93] software_isp: debayer_egl: Bin the raw frame in 2x2
 cells and filter them

The packed Bayer shader reads only the byte holding a pixel's 8 high bits
and ignores the 2 low bits, and an output smaller than the input takes one
pixel in each cell of the scaling, averaging nothing. In the dark, where the
signal is a few tens of counts above black and the tone curve then lifts it
by up to 16x, the 4-count steps and the unaveraged sensor noise are what
shows.

For frames where DebayerParams::denoise is enabled, and an output at most
two thirds of the input's size, work on 2x2 cells of the raw frame instead.
A first pass bins each cell at the full 10 bits into a half-float texel
(bayer_cells_bin.frag: the red, the blue and the mean of the greens, from
six byte fetches), a second filters the cells with a bilateral filter over
their 3x3 neighbourhoods (bayer_cells_denoise.frag) whose range kernel
follows the sensor's noise, a variance of slope * level + quad * level^2 +
floor with widths set by the tuning, and the main pass, a second program of
bayer_1x_packed.frag (CELL_PATH) with the colour processing shared by a
function, samples the filtered cells with the hardware's linear filtering.
It needs half-float render targets (OpenGL ES 3.2, or an extension) and
falls back to the other path without them.

On a synthetic scene with the measured sensor noise, a 1920x1440 output from
4080x3072 has an error against the clean image of 1.8 counts (10-bit) in
flat areas at 16x gain, against 6.3 for the single pixel path, and no more
error on sharp edges, thin lines and fine detail.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
